---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Service",
  "name": "Marketing Automation Consulting",
  "serviceType": "Marketing Automation Consulting",
  "provider": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Pravine Manivannan", "url": "https://pravinemani.com/" },
  "areaServed": "Worldwide",
  "description": "Lead pipelines, lifecycle email, CRM and webhook wiring, and Stripe subscriptions. Code-first marketing infrastructure on Vercel, Resend and Supabase.",
  "url": "https://pravinemani.com/services/marketing-automation/"
};
---

<Base title="Marketing Automation Consulting · Lead Pipelines · Pravine Mani" description="Lead pipelines, lifecycle email, CRM and webhook wiring, and Stripe subscriptions. Code-first marketing infrastructure on Vercel, Resend and Supabase." jsonLd={jsonLd}>

<section class="page-hero">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/services/" style="color: var(--text-dim);">services</a>
    <span style="color: var(--text-muted, #444);">/</span>
    <span class="you">marketing automation</span>
  </div>
  <h1>Marketing automation,<br><span class="accent">in code, without the SaaS tax.</span></h1>
  <p class="lead">Lead capture, CRM wiring, lifecycle email, subscription billing and creative generation, built as code on Vercel, Resend, Supabase and Stripe, with Higgsfield, GPT Image 2 and Nano Banana for media. No Zapier, no Make, no $300/mo platform fee. You own the system outright, and it's debuggable when something breaks.</p>
  <div class="cta-row">
    <a href="/services/" class="cta-btn secondary">All engagement shapes</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="vert-grid">

    <article class="vert-card" id="pipelines">
      <div class="vert-head"><span class="vert-id">01</span><h2>Lead pipelines</h2></div>
      <p>Leads from your paid ads on Facebook and LinkedIn land in your CRM or database within seconds, verified, deduplicated and ready for sales to act on. Nothing gets lost to a flaky Zapier step that no one checks.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Every lead from Meta or LinkedIn Lead Gen reaches your CRM in seconds, not hours</li>
        <li>Cryptographic verification on every webhook, so fake leads can't poison your data</li>
        <li>Retry handling that stops the ad platform from spamming sales with duplicates</li>
        <li>An internal dashboard showing every lead, its source and its follow-up status</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="vert-card" id="email">
      <div class="vert-head"><span class="vert-id">02</span><h2>Lifecycle email</h2></div>
      <p>The right email at the right moment in the customer journey: onboarding, activation, re-engagement and retention. Plain-text, founder-style copy. Subject lines built to earn replies, not opens. It sends from your domain and your reputation.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>An onboarding sequence (Day 2, Day 7, Day 14) tuned to your product and audience</li>
        <li>Subject lines written to earn replies, never the generic "Welcome to..." template</li>
        <li>Bounce and unsubscribe handling that keeps your sender reputation clean</li>
        <li>Open and reply rates surfaced in an internal dashboard so you can see what works</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="vert-card" id="crm">
      <div class="vert-head"><span class="vert-id">03</span><h2>CRM + webhooks</h2></div>
      <p>The wiring between your CRM (HubSpot, Attio, Pipedrive, Salesforce) and the rest of your stack. For small teams, I'll replace the CRM with a custom dashboard that does only what you need, at a fraction of the monthly cost.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Two-way sync that keeps updates consistent across both systems automatically</li>
        <li>One event can trigger many actions (Slack, email, logging, enrichment) without duplicate code</li>
        <li>Real-time Slack alerts on the events that matter: high-fit lead, churn risk, large deal</li>
        <li>For teams under 10, a written recommendation on whether to drop the CRM entirely</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="vert-card" id="stripe">
      <div class="vert-head"><span class="vert-id">04</span><h2>Stripe + subscriptions</h2></div>
      <p>Subscription billing wired up correctly: checkout, a customer self-service portal, failed-payment recovery, annual upgrades and coupons. It lives as code in your repo, not a stack of Zapier zaps that break when Stripe changes a webhook.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Stripe Checkout and the Customer Portal wired so customers self-serve their changes</li>
        <li>A webhook handler that can't miss, double-charge or replay a payment event</li>
        <li>Automatic dunning emails when a payment fails, with grace periods configured</li>
        <li>A revenue dashboard you can check daily: MRR, churn, failed payments and refunds in one view</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="vert-card" id="creative">
      <div class="vert-head"><span class="vert-id">05</span><h2>Creative generation (static)</h2></div>
      <p>For agencies running paid creative at volume: one brief in, dozens of ad image and copy variants out. Brand voice and visual style are locked so the output doesn't drift. Approve, reject and regenerate. One creative round becomes ten testing variants.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A standardized brief format (hook, product, audience, tone) that fits your team's intake</li>
        <li>Brand-voice guardrails so every variant sounds like your client, not generic AI</li>
        <li>An approval workflow where you reject what misses and regenerate without restarting</li>
        <li>A spend ceiling per brief, set up front, that stops the system automatically at your cap</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="vert-card" id="ugc">
      <div class="vert-head"><span class="vert-id">06</span><h2>UGC video generation</h2></div>
      <p>UGC-style ad videos generated from a brief: avatar, product, hook, setting. Output in every aspect ratio (9:16, 1:1, 16:9). It stands in for a small production crew during early testing, so you find a winning hook before paying for a real shoot.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A library of preset on-camera avatars, or custom ones trained from your client's talent</li>
        <li>Product imported automatically from a URL or uploaded images, with no manual modeling</li>
        <li>Hook and scene controlled per variant so each output tests a different ad angle</li>
        <li>A workflow built on the ladder: 4 test variants, pick the winner, scale to 20 of the same</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-head reveal">
    <div class="title-block">
      <div class="label">how it works</div>
      <h2>Engagement shapes for marketing automation.</h2>
      <p>The same paid engagement shapes apply across every focus area. Full ladder at <a href="/services/">/services</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="vert-engagements">
    <a href="/services/#audit" class="vert-eng"><span class="lab">FUNNEL AUDIT</span><span class="when">FIXED-SCOPE WEEK · QUOTE ON SCOPE</span></a>
    <a href="/services/#sprint" class="vert-eng"><span class="lab">PIPELINE SPRINT</span><span class="when">FIXED-SCOPE BUILD · QUOTE ON SCOPE</span></a>
    <a href="/services/#custom" class="vert-eng"><span class="lab">FULL-STACK BUILD</span><span class="when">MULTI-WORKFLOW · MILESTONE-PAID</span></a>
    <a href="/services/#retainer" class="vert-eng"><span class="lab">OPS RETAINER</span><span class="when">MONTHLY · POST-ENGAGEMENT ONLY</span></a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="vert-cta reveal" style="text-align: center;">
    <p style="margin: 0;">A pipeline to scope? <a href="/contact/" style="color: var(--green); border-bottom: 1px dashed var(--green);">Send the brief →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

</Base>
